/* ---------- 2.4.5 Enforcement ---------- */
TOPICS.push({
  id: '2.4.5', unit: 'HR', ref: '2.4.5', title: 'Enforcing human rights',
  short: 'Role of domestic courts under the HRA, judicial review (grounds, procedure, remedies), the European Court of Human Rights',
  summary: 'Rights matter only if they can be enforced. In the UK, individuals can rely on Convention rights in any court under the Human Rights Act, and can challenge public bodies by judicial review. When domestic remedies are exhausted, they can apply to the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg.',
  spec: ['The role of the domestic courts in enforcing human rights', 'The process of judicial review: procedure, standing, grounds and remedies', 'The role of the European Court of Human Rights'],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Domestic courts and the HRA', html: `
<ul><li>Convention rights can be raised in any proceedings — as a claim against a public authority (s7), or as a defence (e.g. Art 10 in a public order prosecution — [[c:ziegler]]).</li>
<li><b>Public authorities</b> (s6): core bodies (government, police, councils, NHS), courts and tribunals, and <b>hybrid</b> bodies when exercising public functions. In <i>YL v Birmingham CC</i> (2007) a private care home was not performing a public function (reversed by statute for care homes in 2008).</li>
<li>Courts interpret legislation compatibly (s3), declare incompatibility (s4), and develop the common law (horizontal effect — [[c:campbellmgn]]).</li>
<li><b>Remedies</b> (s8): quashing orders, injunctions, declarations and damages — damages are modest and awarded only where necessary for “just satisfaction” (<i>R (Greenfield) v SSHD</i>, 2005).</li></ul>` },
    { h: 'Judicial review: procedure', html: `
<p><b>Judicial review</b> is the High Court’s (Administrative Court’s) supervision of the <b>lawfulness</b> of decisions by public bodies — not whether the decision was right on its merits.</p>
<ol><li><b>Pre-action protocol</b> letter.</li><li>Apply for <b>permission</b> (CPR Part 54; Senior Courts Act 1981 s31) — the claim must be arguable.</li><li><b>Time limit</b>: promptly and in any event within <b>three months</b> (six weeks for planning).</li><li><b>Standing</b>: a “sufficient interest” in the matter — interpreted generously for pressure groups (<i>R v Foreign Secretary, ex p World Development Movement</i>, 1995, Pergau Dam). HRA claims need a “victim”.</li><li>Court may refuse relief if the outcome would highly likely not have been substantially different (s31(2A)).</li></ol>` },
    { h: 'Grounds of judicial review', html: `
<p>Classified by Lord Diplock in [[c:gchq]]:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Ground</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Illegality</b></td><td>The decision-maker misunderstood or exceeded its legal powers; improper purpose; irrelevant considerations; fettering discretion</td><td>[[c:fbu]]; [[c:unison]]; <i>Padfield v Minister of Agriculture</i> (1968)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Irrationality</b></td><td>So unreasonable that no reasonable authority could have made it</td><td>[[c:wednesbury]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Procedural impropriety</b></td><td>Failure to follow statutory procedures or natural justice — right to a fair hearing, rule against bias, legitimate expectation</td><td><i>Ridge v Baldwin</i> (1964, chief constable dismissed without a hearing); [[c:pinochet]]; [[c:aylesbury]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Proportionality</b> (for Convention rights)</td><td>Legitimate aim; rational connection; no less intrusive measure; fair balance</td><td><i>R (Daly) v SSHD</i> (2001); <i>Bank Mellat v HM Treasury (No 2)</i> (2013)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Incompatibility with Convention rights</b></td><td>s6 HRA</td><td>[[c:laporte]]</td></tr></table></div>
<h4>Remedies (discretionary)</h4><p><b>Quashing order</b> (with the option since the Judicial Review and Courts Act 2022 of suspending it or making it prospective only), <b>prohibiting order</b>, <b>mandatory order</b>, <b>declaration</b>, <b>injunction</b>, and damages only in limited cases. Parliament sometimes tries to exclude review with <b>ouster clauses</b>, which courts interpret narrowly (<i>R (Privacy International) v IPT</i>, 2019).</p>` },
    { h: 'The European Court of Human Rights', html: `
<ul><li><b>Art 34</b>: individual applications by any person claiming to be a victim of a violation by a member state.</li>
<li><b>Art 35 admissibility</b>: exhaust domestic remedies; apply within <b>four months</b> of the final domestic decision (reduced from six in 2022); not anonymous or manifestly ill-founded; significant disadvantage. The vast majority of applications are rejected at this stage.</li>
<li>Cases are decided by a single judge, committees, chambers of seven, or the <b>Grand Chamber</b> of 17 for serious questions.</li>
<li><b>Art 41</b>: “just satisfaction” — compensation. <b>Art 46</b>: judgments are binding on the state; the <b>Committee of Ministers</b> supervises implementation (e.g. [[c:hirst]] — long delay).</li>
<li><b>Interim measures</b> (Rule 39) in urgent cases of imminent irreparable harm — e.g. stopping the first Rwanda deportation flight in June 2022.</li>
<li>Principles: <b>subsidiarity</b> (national authorities first), <b>margin of appreciation</b>, <b>living instrument</b> (<i>Tyrer v UK</i>, 1978).</li>
<li>Problems: huge backlog (tens of thousands of pending cases); slow; enforcement depends on political will.</li></ul>` }
  ],
  debate: [{ q: 'Is judicial review an effective way to protect rights?', for: ['Holds the government to account — Miller (2019), UNISON, Fire Brigades Union.', 'Generous standing lets pressure groups challenge unlawful policy.', 'Proportionality review under the HRA is more intensive than Wednesbury.', 'Flexible remedies since 2022.'], ag: ['Permission stage, short time limits and costs are barriers.', 'Only reviews legality, not merits — the same decision can be made lawfully again.', 'Legal aid restrictions and costs rules deter claimants.', 'Parliament can limit review (ouster clauses; JR&CA 2022 s2).'] }],
  cases: ['ziegler', 'campbellmgn', 'gchq', 'fbu', 'unison', 'wednesbury', 'pinochet', 'aylesbury', 'laporte', 'hirst'],
  worked: [],
  pitfalls: ['Saying judicial review decides whether a decision was right — it decides whether it was lawful.', 'Forgetting permission, time limits and standing.', 'Getting the Strasbourg time limit wrong — four months since 2022.', 'Saying ECtHR judgments automatically change UK law.'],
  cards: [
    ['What does judicial review examine?', 'The lawfulness of decisions by public bodies, not their merits.'],
    ['Time limit for judicial review?', 'Promptly, and within three months.'],
    ['Three GCHQ grounds?', 'Illegality, irrationality, procedural impropriety (plus proportionality for rights).'],
    ['Ridge v Baldwin (1964)?', 'Dismissal without a hearing breached natural justice.'],
    ['Bank Mellat (2013) proportionality test?', 'Important objective; rational connection; less intrusive means; fair balance.'],
    ['Judicial review remedies?', 'Quashing, prohibiting and mandatory orders; declaration; injunction; limited damages.'],
    ['Art 34 ECHR?', 'Individual applications to Strasbourg.'],
    ['Art 35 time limit?', 'Four months from the final domestic decision (since 2022).'],
    ['Art 46 ECHR?', 'States must abide by judgments; Committee of Ministers supervises.'],
    ['What is a Rule 39 interim measure?', 'Urgent order to prevent imminent irreparable harm — e.g. Rwanda flight 2022.'],
    ['YL v Birmingham CC (2007)?', 'Private care home not a public authority (reversed by statute).']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Judicial review is concerned with whether a decision was…', o: ['lawful', 'the best decision', 'popular', 'cheap'], x: 'Not merits.' },
    { q: 'Which case set out the grounds of illegality, irrationality and procedural impropriety?', o: ['CCSU v Minister for the Civil Service (GCHQ)', 'Wednesbury', 'Ridge v Baldwin', 'Daly'], x: '1985.' },
    { q: 'A claim for judicial review must normally be brought within…', o: ['three months', 'six years', 'one month', 'one year'], x: 'Promptly.' },
    { q: 'Applications to the ECtHR must be made within…', o: ['four months of the final domestic decision', 'six years', 'one month', 'there is no time limit'], x: 'Protocol 15.' },
    { q: 'Who supervises the execution of ECtHR judgments?', o: ['The Committee of Ministers', 'The UK Supreme Court', 'The European Commission', 'The UN'], x: 'Art 46.' },
    { q: 'An order cancelling an unlawful decision is a…', o: ['quashing order', 'mandatory order', 'prohibiting order', 'declaration'], x: 'Formerly certiorari.' }
  ],
  exam: [{ q: 'Analyse and evaluate the effectiveness of the methods of enforcing human rights in the UK. [25]', m: 25, ms: ['HRA domestic enforcement — ss3, 4, 6, 7, 8', 'hybrid public authorities — YL', 'judicial review — procedure and barriers', 'grounds — GCHQ; proportionality (Daly, Bank Mellat)', 'remedies — discretionary; 2022 reforms', 'ECtHR — admissibility, backlog', 'implementation — Hirst', 'interim measures controversy', 'costs and legal aid', 'conclusion'] }],
  tools: ['jrtree']
});

/* ---------- 2.4.6 Reform ---------- */
TOPICS.push({
  id: '2.4.6', unit: 'HR', ref: '2.4.6', title: 'Reform of human rights protection',
  short: 'Reform of the protection of human rights, the need for a UK Bill of Rights, the Equality and Human Rights Commission',
  summary: 'Few areas of law are as politically contested as human rights. This topic covers the arguments about reforming the Human Rights Act, the proposals for a UK Bill of Rights (including the 2022 Bill), the debate about leaving the ECHR, and the role of the Equality and Human Rights Commission.',
  spec: ['Reform of the protection of human rights in the UK', 'The need for a UK Bill of Rights', 'The role of the Equality and Human Rights Commission'],
  learn: [
    { h: 'The reform debate', html: `
<p>Reform proposals fall into three groups:</p>
<ol><li><b>Keep the HRA</b>, perhaps with changes to procedure — the <b>Independent Human Rights Act Review</b> (chaired by Sir Peter Gross, 2021) found the Act had worked well and recommended only modest changes.</li>
<li><b>Replace the HRA with a UK Bill of Rights</b> while staying in the ECHR.</li>
<li><b>Leave the ECHR</b> altogether and rely on domestic law — proposed by some politicians, including, from 2025, the Conservative Party and Reform UK.</li></ol>
<p>Others argue for <b>stronger</b> protection: an entrenched written constitution, more economic and social rights, or giving courts power to strike down legislation.</p>` },
    { h: 'A UK Bill of Rights', html: `
<p><b>History</b>: promised in Conservative manifestos from 2006; the <b>Commission on a Bill of Rights</b> (2012) could not reach consensus. The <b>Bill of Rights Bill 2022</b> would have:</p>
<ul><li>repealed the HRA while keeping Convention rights;</li><li>removed the s3 duty to interpret legislation compatibly;</li><li>stated that the UK Supreme Court is the ultimate judicial arbiter, and courts need not follow Strasbourg;</li><li>told courts to disregard Strasbourg interim measures;</li><li>added a permission stage requiring claimants to show “significant disadvantage”;</li><li>limited Art 8 arguments against deporting foreign criminals, and given extra weight to freedom of speech.</li></ul>
<p>It was <b>withdrawn in June 2023</b>, after criticism from Parliament’s Joint Committee on Human Rights, lawyers and devolved governments.</p>
<div class="debate"><div class="for"><h5>For a UK Bill of Rights</h5><ul><li>A “British” document with greater public ownership and democratic legitimacy</li><li>Could restore the balance between Parliament, courts and Strasbourg</li><li>Could add rights (e.g. jury trial, free speech emphasis)</li><li>Clarity about responsibilities as well as rights</li></ul></div><div class="ag"><h5>Against</h5><ul><li>The HRA already preserves sovereignty and works (Gross Review)</li><li>Would weaken protection and increase litigation in Strasbourg</li><li>Problems for devolution and the Belfast (Good Friday) Agreement</li><li>Damage to the UK’s international reputation</li><li>Rights for everyone should not depend on political mood</li></ul></div></div>` },
    { h: 'Leaving the ECHR?', html: `
<p>Those in favour argue that Strasbourg blocks immigration and deportation policy and overrides Parliament. Those against point to the ECHR’s role in the Belfast (Good Friday) Agreement and the UK–EU Trade and Cooperation Agreement (co-operation on policing depends on ECHR membership), the UK’s role in drafting it, and the risk of weakening rights protection for everyone. The only states to have left the Council of Europe are Greece under military rule (1969, later rejoining) and Russia (expelled in 2022). The government elected in 2024 committed to staying in the ECHR.</p>` },
    { h: 'The Equality and Human Rights Commission', html: `
<p>Created by the <b>Equality Act 2006</b> (operating from 2007), the EHRC is Great Britain’s national equality body and a national human rights institution. (Scotland has its own Scottish Human Rights Commission; Northern Ireland has the NI Human Rights Commission and Equality Commission.)</p>
<ul><li><b>Duties</b>: promote understanding of equality and human rights; encourage good practice; work to eliminate discrimination; monitor progress.</li>
<li><b>Powers</b>: inquiries and formal <b>investigations</b>; <b>unlawful act notices</b> and binding agreements; issuing statutory <b>codes of practice</b> and guidance on the Equality Act 2010; assisting individuals with legal cases; bringing <b>judicial review</b> and intervening in cases.</li>
<li><b>Examples</b>: inquiries into racial harassment in universities, investigations into the Labour Party (antisemitism, 2020); guidance following <i>For Women Scotland v Scottish Ministers</i> (2025), where the Supreme Court held that “sex” in the Equality Act 2010 means biological sex.</li>
<li><b>Criticisms</b>: large budget cuts since 2010; concerns about independence and appointments; questions about its accreditation status as a human rights institution; controversy over its guidance on sex and gender.</li></ul>` }
  ],
  debate: [{ q: 'Should the Human Rights Act be replaced by a British Bill of Rights?', for: ['Greater democratic ownership and legitimacy.', 'Clarifies relationship with Strasbourg — UK courts supreme.', 'Could reduce controversial deportation outcomes.', 'Responsibilities alongside rights.'], ag: ['Gross Review: HRA works well.', 'Bill of Rights Bill 2022 widely criticised and withdrawn.', 'Would reduce protection and increase Strasbourg applications.', 'Devolution and Good Friday Agreement complications.', 'Uncertainty and cost of change.'] }],
  cases: ['hirst', 'othman', 'belmarsh'],
  worked: [],
  pitfalls: ['Writing as if the Bill of Rights Bill 2022 became law.', 'Confusing the EHRC with the ECtHR.', 'Ignoring devolution and the Belfast Agreement in reform essays.'],
  cards: [
    ['Independent Human Rights Act Review (2021)?', 'Chaired by Sir Peter Gross; found the HRA working well; modest changes.'],
    ['Commission on a Bill of Rights (2012)?', 'Could not reach consensus.'],
    ['Bill of Rights Bill 2022 — fate?', 'Withdrawn June 2023.'],
    ['Two key features of the 2022 Bill?', 'Repeal HRA and remove s3; UK Supreme Court as ultimate arbiter; ignore interim measures.'],
    ['Which Act created the EHRC?', 'Equality Act 2006.'],
    ['Three EHRC powers?', 'Inquiries and investigations; unlawful act notices; judicial review and interventions; codes of practice.'],
    ['For Women Scotland v Scottish Ministers (2025)?', '“Sex” in the Equality Act 2010 means biological sex.'],
    ['Why does leaving the ECHR affect Northern Ireland?', 'The Belfast (Good Friday) Agreement requires ECHR incorporation.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'The Bill of Rights Bill 2022 was…', o: ['withdrawn in 2023', 'enacted in 2023', 'rejected by the Supreme Court', 'approved by referendum'], x: 'Never passed.' },
    { q: 'The Equality and Human Rights Commission was created by…', o: ['the Equality Act 2006', 'the Human Rights Act 1998', 'the Equality Act 2010', 'the Constitutional Reform Act 2005'], x: 'Began 2007.' },
    { q: 'The Independent Human Rights Act Review (2021) concluded that…', o: ['the HRA had worked well, needing only modest reform', 'the HRA should be repealed', 'the UK should leave the ECHR', 'judges should strike down Acts'], x: 'Gross Review.' },
    { q: 'Which is a power of the EHRC?', o: ['Issuing unlawful act notices', 'Striking down Acts of Parliament', 'Hearing criminal appeals', 'Appointing judges'], x: 'Equality Act 2006.' },
    { q: 'A key argument against leaving the ECHR concerns…', o: ['the Belfast (Good Friday) Agreement', 'the Magna Carta', 'the Theft Act', 'the Juries Act'], x: 'And TCA policing co-operation.' }
  ],
  exam: [{ q: 'Analyse and evaluate whether the Human Rights Act 1998 should be replaced with a UK Bill of Rights. [25]', m: 25, ms: ['current position — HRA dialogue model', 'criticisms of HRA — Hirst, Othman, s3', 'Bill of Rights proposals — 2012 Commission; 2022 Bill contents', 'withdrawal 2023; Gross Review', 'arguments for — legitimacy, sovereignty', 'arguments against — reduced protection, devolution, GFA', 'leaving ECHR debate', 'role of EHRC / JCHR', 'international reputation', 'conclusion'] }],
  tools: ['reformsorthr']
});

